#lang saffron
;; The other half of saffron-unannotated-container-param: grounding an OPEN
;; type argument to `any` must not ground a DETERMINED one.
;;
;; `takes-int-vec` declares `(Vec int)`, which has nothing open, so the seam
;; leaves it alone and checks against it -- a `(Vec any)` holding a cstr is
;; refused, exactly as before the fix.  Without this fixture, a later change
;; that grounded every type argument (rather than only the undetermined ones)
;; would turn the seam into a no-op and nothing would notice: the sibling
;; fixture would still pass, because it only exercises the open case.
;;
;; `hide` launders the literal through an unannotated parameter so the
;; mismatch is a RUNTIME one -- the whole point of a checked seam.  Passing
;; the literal directly would be caught statically and prove nothing about it.
;;
;; "before" is printed first so the fixture distinguishes "panicked at the
;; seam" from "never ran".

(defn takes-int-vec [v : (Vec int)] (vec-get v 0))
(defn hide [x] x)

(defn main []
  (println "before")
  (println (cast (takes-int-vec (hide ["nope"])) int))
  0)
